# What They Really Think videos

`docs/custom-videos.md` is the production system. Read it and the matching page under `docs/videos/` before generating or rewriting a cut, then follow its execution checklist exactly.

- Start from a **title**. Canonical Cloud command: `python -m channel generate --channel <mode> --title "…"`. Sequential local: `python -m channel init`. Do not hardcode a person into `channel/config.py` or write a new runner. Jobs live in `artifacts/`. A second mode, `behind_the_business` (YouTube: **How They Really Make Money**), is documented in `docs/behind-the-business.md`. A third mode, `how_they_took_over` (YouTube: **How They Took Over**), is documented in `docs/how-they-took-over.md`. Do not apply those story, length, or visual rules to a What They Really Think title. Pass `--channel` explicitly. Behind The Business scripts are **4400–5500** words at Kokoro **1.15** (never below 1.0 on a new title). How They Took Over scripts are **2800–3600** words at Kokoro **1.15**. `DO NOT MODIFY THE VIDEO ENGINE` during a normal generation task.
- Sacred for every video: **Fresh research for every video.** **Different story architecture** for each company. **Original narration, not rewritten articles or YouTube transcripts.** **Unique scenes and diagrams** built around that company's actual business.
- The point is to **answer the title through a story**. Not a Wikipedia list, not a numbered lecture, not a biography dump.
- Each title needs a **unique story engine** (one object, one place, one reversal that would not work on any other cut). Read `docs/videos/` first. Do not reuse chapter cards, a too-close `the_thought`, a summoning circle, a tortoise/hare, an inbox/check, or a "Month Year. Name…" cold open. `lint_story.py` fails those. **originality_score** vs the last 10 videos must be ≥ 80 and **`ready_to_publish`** before GenerateImage (`python -m channel qa`, `scripts/lint_originality.py`). If you could swap the names and keep the same video, reject it.
- Spine is a **blunt simple cartoon** a five-year-old can retell and an adult still enjoys. Speak real names (OpenAI, Grok, SpaceX). Keep those names out of image prompts.
- Research through today. **Do not say today's date** in the VO (`as of today`, `today is August 22`, `this morning`). Date events with months and years.
- `the_thought` is the title payoff — one sentence a child could repeat, said in the VO. Narration around it is an intelligent friend telling history, not baby talk.
- Cursor Grok fills research / story / bibles / scenes in `channel/projects/<slug>/project.json`. Compile writes fixtures. Images are **GenerateImage** (Grok). Voice is Kokoro (default `am_liam`; new titles may rotate `am_michael` / `am_fenrir`). Never Edge, never ElevenLabs.
- Character consistency is the bible `visual_lock`. Historical personal names stay out of image prompts. Global style is frozen in `channel/config.py`.
- Prop economy: ONE signature prop in ≤ 6 scenes. The linter enforces it — fix errors before generating any image. When the prop returns it is the **same obvious object** (high contrast, large in frame), not a faint mark.
- Long cuts are **~20–25 minutes** (4400–5500 words at Kokoro `am_liam` 1.15). Spoken English, third person. Calendar years as digits (`1995`) in the fixture and captions; Kokoro pronounces them as years. Silent black chapter cards. Stylish burned-in captions on narrated scenes (Shorts captions sit in the YouTube safe band, never hugging the bottom edge). Fill-frame 16:9. Every Short punches in the first two seconds and ends on “Watch the full video. The link is in the description.” (branded card). After compile, GenerateImage the 16:9 and 9:16 thumbnail jobs (no on-image text) and run `python -m channel youtube <slug>` for description, tags, 1280×720, and 1080×1920 JPEGs. YouTube descriptions include an honest synthetic-media disclosure. Shorts description is exactly: `Watch the full video:` / `https://youtu.be/<id>` / blank line / `<short_title>. <hook>` / blank line / disclosure. Set `youtube.full_video_url` after the long video is live. Different titles wait 24 hours between assembles. Tick YouTube Studio's altered/synthetic content checkbox on upload.
- When a What They Really Think video ships, update `docs/videos/<slug>.md` and `docs/videos/README.md`. When a Behind The Business video ships, update `docs/business/<slug>.md` and `docs/business/README.md`. When a How They Took Over video ships, update `docs/takeover/<slug>.md` and `docs/takeover/README.md`.
- Shared contract: chat is not the source of truth. After any production change (captions, YouTube copy, voice, length, story rules, image brands, thumbs), update `docs/custom-videos.md`, `AGENTS.md`, and this rule in the **same commit**, plus a linter/test if the rule is checkable, plus the shipped `docs/videos/` page and fixtures. Then push so a new environment can `git pull` the same type of video. Never leave a rule only in conversation. `tests/test_channel_handoff.py` fails when these surfaces drift.
